  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A nice end to the Labor Day weekend.\n\nA refreshingly low dewpoint airmass has settled in across \nPennsylvania and will ensure pleasant conditions for Labor Day \noutdoor activities. \n\n   ----------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Uptick in heat and humidity for midweek.\n\nAs the center of high pressure drifts southeast of PA on Tuesday\nand Wednesday, return southwesterly flow will usher in an uptick\nand heat and humidity. The never ending ridge of high pressure\ncentered over the Central US will also begin to nudge into the\nMid Atlantic as upper troughing over the Northeast retreats and\nthe amplified upper level pattern begins to flatten out a bit. \n\nHighs on Tuesday will range from the mid 70s to mid 80s and \nWednesday will trend about 5 degrees warmer areawide. Combined \nwith dewpoints back in the mid to upper 60s, heat index values \nmay crest the 90F mark in the south central mountains on \nWednesday. Dry conditions will prevail and we'll continue to \nenjoy a break from very active stretch of severe weather we've \ndealt with this summer.\n\n   ----------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Showers, storms, and gusty winds Wednesday night \ninto Thursday.\n\nBy Wednesday night, a low pressure system will be moving across\nnorthern Ontario with a cold front extending south through the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio Valley region. The pressure gradient \nwill support a notable uptick in synoptic scale winds Wednesday\nafternoon into Thursday. It's been awhile since we've had \nwidespread gusty winds, but we're getting into the time of year \nwhere such a weather pattern becomes more common. \n\nGusty winds will ramp up Wednesday during the day out of the\nsouthwest ahead of an approaching cold front, and continue\nthrough Thursday afternoon shifting to a westerly direction \nafter the front passes. Wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph seem \nrealistic at this point, with the highest readings occurring at\nhigher elevations in the Laurel Highlands.\n\nThe cold front will also bring a chance for showers and storms\nahead of and along the front. The risk for severe weather will\ndepend on the magnitude of a shortwave trough racing through \nthe region and any associated jet dynamics in play. \n\nVertical wind profiles look unidirectional, so the primary \nhazard would be from damaging winds. Timing of the cold front\nand dewpoints largely staying out of the upper 60s to lower\n70s range are the 2 main reasons to think storms will not get\ntoo crazy, unlike recent events. Timing of the cold front has\nbeen similar the last few days.\n\nRain arrives in northwest PA late Wednesday night and will sag\nsouth across the Commonwealth during the day on Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand sunny Friday. There could be a few isolated PM showers and a\nstorm or two on Saturday or Sunday, but most locations will stay\ndry.",
